 ## Score levels
 
-**6 (very rare):** A strong 5 plus a clear sense of audience, an acknowledged counterpoint answered with reasons, or genuine synthesis across sources, sustained throughout.
+**6 (very rare):** A strong 5 plus a clear sense of audience, an acknowledged counterpoint answered with reasons, or genuine synthesis across sources, sustained throughout. A long essay that repeats "quote + own explanation" in every advantage paragraph and also answers objections or addresses the reader directly can be a 6 even if the introduction is loose or errors are frequent.
 
-**5 (strong):** Thesis (often previewing advantages) in an introduction, a real conclusion, and body paragraphs that name benefits (health, cleaner air, less stress, savings, community improvement). Draws rich, mostly accurate detail (statistics, quotes, examples) from three or four sources, with several specifics per source. Paragraphs may be organized by advantage OR by source/place, as long as benefits are repeatedly stated. Explanation may be brief or repetitive, and there may be misreadings or many errors. Do NOT lower a framed, detail-rich, multi-source essay to 4 only because the explanation is "uneven" or "thin" or because one source is unused or one detail is misread. Likewise, a well-organized essay with advantage topic paragraphs, parenthetical source citations, and sustained reasoning from 3 sources is a 5.
+**5 (strong):** Thesis (often previewing advantages) in an introduction, a real conclusion, and body paragraphs that name benefits (health, cleaner air, less stress, savings, community improvement). Draws rich, mostly accurate detail (statistics, quotes, examples) from three or four sources. Paragraphs may be organized by advantage OR by source/place, as long as benefits are repeatedly stated and the details are plentiful. **Strong 5 signal:** an introduction that previews advantages, advantage-themed body paragraphs that each cite evidence from different sources with explicit labels like "(Source 1)", and a conclusion. Essays with this pattern are 5s even if explanation is brief, the conclusion only restates the thesis, one source is unused, or a detail is misread. Do NOT lower a framed, detail-rich, multi-source essay to 4 only because the explanation is "uneven" or "thin." A 5 still requires that most details are tied to a named benefit.
 
-**4 (competent):** Clear thesis, introduction, body paragraphs built around distinct reasons or advantages (or sources, if benefits are explained), a conclusion, and the writer explaining reasons in their own words. Source support may be thinner (one or two sources, fewer details), but the logic is easy to follow. Also a 4: an opinion-and-reasons essay with no sources but with intro, several distinct advantages each developed in its own paragraph, and a conclusion. Uneven explanation, errors, or few sources do not push such an essay down to 3.
+**4 (competent):** Clear thesis, introduction, body paragraphs built around distinct reasons or advantages (or sources, if benefits are explained), a conclusion, and the writer explaining reasons in their own words. Source support may be thinner (one or two sources, fewer details), but the logic is easy to follow. If these four elements are present, the essay is a 4 even with many errors, uneven explanation, or quotes mixed in. Also a 4: an opinion-and-reasons essay with no sources but with intro, several distinct advantages each developed in its own paragraph, and a conclusion. Advantage paragraphs that drift into reporting trends or events, with little link back to why it is an advantage, stay at 3–4, not 5.
 
-**3 (developing):** A recognizable position and some relevant support, with paragraphs that have topics, but explanation of why details are advantages is only occasional. Mostly paraphrased source facts with a few real comments, generic or repetitive reasoning, loose organization, or a weak or missing introduction or conclusion. Source-by-source paragraphs with only one or two details per source and brief reactions belong at 2–3 (3 only if most paragraphs clearly state why the details are advantages).
+**3 (developing):** A recognizable position and some relevant support, with paragraphs that have topics, but explanation of why details are advantages is only occasional. Mostly paraphrased source facts with a few real comments, generic or repetitive reasoning, loose organization, or a weak or missing introduction or conclusion. Use 3 only when one of the 4-level elements (framing, advantage paragraphs, own-word reasons, conclusion) is clearly missing or weak. Source-by-source paragraphs with only one or two details per source and brief reactions belong at 2–3 (3 only if most paragraphs clearly state why the details are advantages).
 
-**2 (limited):** Mainly reports what sources say, usually one paragraph per source, with only short closing remarks or brief reactions. Thesis is vague; the conclusion is generic or absent. This applies even with an intro and conclusion, three or four sources, quotes with citations, and clean writing, if the details per source are few and the commentary is one line. Also a 2: a brief or rambling opinion with a few ideas and little support, or source fragments plus a slight own point.
+**2 (limited):** Mainly reports what sources say, usually one paragraph per source (often introduced by article title or author name rather than by an advantage), with only short closing remarks or brief reactions. Thesis is vague; the conclusion is generic or absent. This applies even with an intro and conclusion, three or four sources, quotes, and clean writing, if the details per source are few and the commentary is one line. Also a 2: a brief or rambling opinion with a few ideas and little support, or source fragments plus a slight own point, or an own-words opinion that misreads the sources.
 
 **1 (minimal):** No real argument or explanation.
 - Copied or near-copied source sentences, or disconnected facts listed one after another, with no thesis/intro/conclusion and no explanation (even if all four sources are touched).
